#X obj 674 11 file.size;
#X obj 230 78 cnv 1 535 442 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -237178 -1 0;
#X text 240 83 The object outputs the size of the specified file in
bytes. By default the request is performed asynchronously \, so there
is usually a short delay between the input and the output. Set @async
to 0 (or use the @sync alias) to make the request synchronous.;
#X text 240 168 Why asynchronous by default: file system requests can
take an unpredictable amount of time — from tens of microseconds up
to hundreds of milliseconds on network drives \, external media or
systems with antivirus filtering. Pd processes audio in real time \,
and each DSP block must be computed within a strict time budget. If
a slow file operation were performed synchronously in the main thread
\, it could stall audio processing and cause dropouts \, clicks or
even a temporary audio freeze. Running the request in a background
thread avoids this: the main thread continues to process audio and
messages \, and the result is sent to the outlet as soon as it is available.;
#X text 240 374 When to use synchronous mode: if the patch logic requires
the result to be available immediately after sending the request (for
example \, when the next step depends on whether the file exists or
how large it is) \, set @async to 0 or use the @sync alias. In this
case the object returns the value synchronously \, but you accept the
risk that a slow file operation may block the main thread.;
